1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,233
Ten seconds to atmosphere.

2
00:00:02,593 --> 00:00:06,622
Velocity, one hundred and seventy thousand
kilometres an hour.

3
00:00:06,982 --> 00:00:11,102
There's no ground down there, nothing to
land on, just down.

4
00:00:11,812 --> 00:00:14,987
In 1995, a machine fell into Jupiter.

5
00:00:15,347 --> 00:00:18,735
For fifty-eight minutes, it sent back
everything it saw.

6
00:00:19,095 --> 00:00:22,305
Then the pressure silenced it forever.

7
00:00:22,665 --> 00:00:24,527
Nothing has gone deeper since.

8
00:00:24,887 --> 00:00:27,613
This is what happens if you keep falling.

9
00:00:27,973 --> 00:00:29,570
Past the place where it died.

10
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:31,232
All the way to the centre.

11
00:00:31,942 --> 00:00:33,231
Here we go.

12
00:00:34,731 --> 00:00:37,230
Contact, contact, everything's shaking!

13
00:00:37,590 --> 00:00:40,568
The window, the window is on fire!

14
00:00:41,278 --> 00:00:45,712
At this speed, the gas in front of the
capsule can't get out of the way.

15
00:00:46,072 --> 00:00:50,316
It piles up into a shock wave and turns
into glowing plasma.

16
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:52,391
Sixteen thousand degrees.

17
00:00:52,751 --> 00:00:55,967
Almost three times hotter than the surface
of the Sun.

18
00:00:56,327 --> 00:00:57,726
Then the braking hits.

19
00:00:58,086 --> 00:01:02,829
For a few seconds, Mara weighs two hundred
and twenty-eight times her normal weight.

20
00:01:03,189 --> 00:01:05,395
More than fifteen tonnes.

21
00:01:05,755 --> 00:01:07,665
No human could survive that.

22
00:01:08,025 --> 00:01:09,447
The real probe did.

23
00:01:09,807 --> 00:01:10,965
It was built to.

24
00:01:11,675 --> 00:01:12,867
I'm still here.

25
00:01:13,227 --> 00:01:14,612
I think I blacked out.

26
00:01:14,972 --> 00:01:16,238
The parachute is open.

27
00:01:17,738 --> 00:01:21,427
I'm at the cloud tops, and it's minus one
hundred and sixty outside.

28
00:01:21,787 --> 00:01:22,907
Everything is white.

29
00:01:23,617 --> 00:01:28,770
She's falling through the top of Jupiter's
weather: three layers of cloud, stacked

30
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:30,906
about seventy-one kilometres deep.

31
00:01:31,266 --> 00:01:33,078
Ammonia ice on top.

32
00:01:33,438 --> 00:01:35,774
Ammonium hydrosulfide below it.

33
00:01:36,134 --> 00:01:37,917
And at the bottom, water.

34
00:01:38,627 --> 00:01:42,353
Something's flashing beneath me, it's
lightning, and it's enormous.

35
00:01:43,063 --> 00:01:45,295
Jupiter's water clouds make lightning.

36
00:01:45,655 --> 00:01:50,541
And Juno spotted something even stranger:
hail made of ammonia and water slush,

37
00:01:50,541 --> 00:01:52,149
falling out of the storms.

38
00:01:52,509 --> 00:01:54,920
Scientists call them mushballs.

39
00:01:55,630 --> 00:01:57,482
Wind just hit us sideways.

40
00:01:57,842 --> 00:02:01,051
I'm reading six hundred and forty metres
per second.

41
00:02:01,761 --> 00:02:03,475
That's what the real probe measured.

42
00:02:03,835 --> 00:02:06,682
Almost twice the speed of sound on Earth.

43
00:02:07,392 --> 00:02:09,584
It's getting darker, and warmer.

44
00:02:11,084 --> 00:02:15,786
About an hour after entry, Mara reaches a
very special place.

45
00:02:16,146 --> 00:02:21,168
In 1995, NASA's Galileo probe stopped
transmitting right here.

46
00:02:21,528 --> 00:02:23,745
About a hundred and eighty kilometres
down.

47
00:02:24,105 --> 00:02:26,901
Twenty-two times the pressure of Earth's
air.

48
00:02:27,261 --> 00:02:31,089
Around a hundred and fifty degrees, hotter
than boiling water.

49
00:02:31,449 --> 00:02:33,858
Its last signal came from this depth.

50
00:02:34,218 --> 00:02:35,744
Then, silence.

51
00:02:36,454 --> 00:02:38,814
Passing the depth where Galileo went
silent.

52
00:02:39,174 --> 00:02:41,615
There's nothing here, just dark.

53
00:02:41,975 --> 00:02:46,765
From now on, nobody has ever seen this,
and I'm the first person to see it.

54
00:02:47,475 --> 00:02:50,867
Below this point, everything we know comes
from the outside.

55
00:02:51,227 --> 00:02:55,805
From Juno, a spacecraft that has been
looping around Jupiter since 2016,

56
00:02:55,805 --> 00:03:00,066
weighing the planet with gravity and
listening to it in microwaves.

57
00:03:01,566 --> 00:03:05,387
I'm two hundred kilometres down, and the
wind still hasn't stopped.

58
00:03:06,097 --> 00:03:07,776
It won't for a long time.

59
00:03:08,136 --> 00:03:13,198
Juno found that Jupiter's famous stripes
are jet streams that reach about three

60
00:03:13,198 --> 00:03:14,800
thousand kilometres deep.

61
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:20,421
And the Great Red Spot, a storm twice as
wide as Earth, has roots about five

62
00:03:20,421 --> 00:03:22,083
hundred kilometres down.

63
00:03:22,443 --> 00:03:27,305
Galileo's entire journey would fit inside
it almost three times.

64
00:03:28,805 --> 00:03:30,484
Pressure's climbing too fast.

65
00:03:30,844 --> 00:03:32,419
The hull is groaning.

66
00:03:32,779 --> 00:03:36,232
Wait, the outside looks thick and heavy,
like we're under water.

67
00:03:36,942 --> 00:03:38,144
There's no splash.

68
00:03:38,504 --> 00:03:39,483
No surface.

69
00:03:39,843 --> 00:03:44,707
Hydrogen, almost ninety percent of
Jupiter, is squeezed so hard that it

70
00:03:44,707 --> 00:03:47,310
slowly turns from a gas into a liquid.

71
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:49,373
Mara isn't falling anymore.

72
00:03:49,733 --> 00:03:50,626
She's sinking.

73
00:03:51,336 --> 00:03:52,669
I'm in an ocean.

74
00:03:53,029 --> 00:03:54,802
An ocean bigger than Earth.

75
00:03:55,512 --> 00:04:00,352
Roughly halfway to the centre, the
pressure gets so extreme that it rips

76
00:04:00,352 --> 00:04:02,570
electrons off the hydrogen atoms.

77
00:04:02,930 --> 00:04:06,641
The ocean starts to conduct electricity,
like liquid metal.

78
00:04:07,351 --> 00:04:10,293
Every compass on board is spinning.

79
00:04:10,653 --> 00:04:12,432
There's current everywhere.

80
00:04:13,142 --> 00:04:18,644
This metal ocean, churning inside a planet
that spins once every ten hours, is a

81
00:04:18,644 --> 00:04:19,538
giant dynamo.

82
00:04:19,898 --> 00:04:24,393
It powers a magnetic field up to
fifty-four times stronger than Earth's,

83
00:04:24,393 --> 00:04:28,950
the same field that traps the intense
radiation the real probe had to fly

84
00:04:28,950 --> 00:04:30,323
through on its way in.

85
00:04:31,823 --> 00:04:33,601
I've lost the link to the orbiter.

86
00:04:33,961 --> 00:04:35,194
Recording anyway.

87
00:04:35,904 --> 00:04:40,180
For decades, textbooks drew a small, solid
rock at Jupiter's centre.

88
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:44,191
Juno's gravity measurements say that
picture is wrong.

89
00:04:44,551 --> 00:04:46,750
The core is huge, and fuzzy.

90
00:04:47,110 --> 00:04:49,575
Partly dissolved into the metal around it.

91
00:04:49,935 --> 00:04:54,071
In some models, it stretches more than
halfway to the cloud tops.

92
00:04:54,431 --> 00:04:59,742
One explanation: when Jupiter was young, a
planet about ten times the mass of Earth

93
00:04:59,742 --> 00:05:04,990
may have hit it head-on, shattering its
core so badly that it never settled again.

94
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:09,696
There's no floor and no rock, it just gets
denser and hotter.

95
00:05:10,406 --> 00:05:15,726
Near the centre, scientists estimate the
temperature at around twenty-four thousand

96
00:05:15,726 --> 00:05:16,239
degrees.

97
00:05:16,599 --> 00:05:20,147
More than four times hotter than the
surface of the Sun.

98
00:05:20,857 --> 00:05:23,423
This is Vesper, final log.

99
00:05:23,783 --> 00:05:26,432
If anyone ever hears this, it was
beautiful.

100
00:05:27,932 --> 00:05:29,635
Mara's journey was imagined.

101
00:05:29,995 --> 00:05:31,594
The probe's was not.

102
00:05:31,954 --> 00:05:37,015
In 1995, a small machine really did fall
into the biggest planet in our solar

103
00:05:37,015 --> 00:05:41,090
system, and for fifty-eight minutes, it
told us what it found.

104
00:05:41,450 --> 00:05:46,341
Everything deeper, we've pieced together
since, from far outside, and every number

105
00:05:46,341 --> 00:05:48,250
you heard along the way is real.

106
00:05:48,610 --> 00:05:50,761
A place no one will ever see.

107
00:05:51,121 --> 00:05:52,384
But we know it's there.

108
00:05:52,744 --> 00:05:56,353
If you want to fall into another world
with us, subscribe.
